erministic structure—which AIRs are foldable and their dimensions are fixed after round 0, so we're only swapping data pointers during the ping-pong pattern rather than rebuilding the entire structure each time.\n\nThe trace commit phase is spending 246ms, and a big chunk of that comes from building 624 separate Merkle trees sequentially with no multi-stream parallelism. Each tree goes through stacking, RS encoding, Poseidon2 row hashing, and multi-layer compression as individual kernel launches. Parallelizing tree construction across streams could significantly reduce this overhead.\n\nFor the GKR fractional sumcheck portion, we're looking at roughly 300-400ms out of the total 527ms GKR time. There are 20-21 rounds with 0-22 sumcheck inner loops each, and since each round depends on the previous challenge, they have to run sequentially. The precompute-M optimization helps by batching 3 rounds into a single multifold, reducing total kernel launches, but the fundamental dependency chain still limits parallelism.\n\nThe real bottleneck is the ~400 kernel launches paired with device-to-host syncs. The sequential nature of the algorithm makes this hard to parallelize away.\n\nLooking at potential improvements, pre-computing the MLE round descriptors could save around 50ms by building the static parts of fold descriptor arrays upfront for all rounds, then just updating data pointers during the loop. This eliminates per-round CPU descriptor construction and might let us batch H2D uploads together. The descriptor structure is deterministic, so this should be medium-high probability with moderate complexity.\n\nFor the Merkle tree construction in trace commit, using multi-stream parallelism could help significantly. Looking into whether `calculate_zero_hash` is actually in this codebase or if it's buried in a dependency like Plonky3. For the MLE rounds optimization, I'm reconsidering whether the precompute-M windowing technique even applies here—it's specifically for the fr