and computes sum polynomials, and something about that computation is getting slower rather than faster with the reduced workload.\n\nLooking at the two main operations: fold_mle_evals builds descriptor arrays and launches a kernel to process all 623 AIRs, while sumcheck_polys_batch_eval collects pointers, uploads descriptors, and launches interpolation and evaluation kernels. Both scale with the number of AIRs, but the CPU overhead of managing 623 separate AIRs through descriptor arrays and kernel launches might be dominating the actual computation time.\n\nEstimating the CPU cost: if each AIR requires 3-5 microseconds per round for pointer collection and descriptor building, then across 623 AIRs and 24 rounds, that's roughly 60 milliseconds of pure CPU overhead just managing the batch. At lower APC values with fewer AIRs, this overhead scales down proportionally.\n\nThe key insight is to pre-compute everything possible before entering the MLE loop—the fold patterns, buffer offsets, descriptor arrays, height/width progressions, case classifications for sumcheck evaluation, and interpolation metadata—so the loop itself only handles the actual data pointers that change with each ping-pong swap.\n\nThis restructuring would cut out roughly 60-80ms of overhead: the per-round per-AIR CPU iteration work and the repeated H2D uploads that currently happen multiple times per round across all 24 rounds.\n\nNow I'm looking at the kernel execution times within the MLE Rounds phase to see where else I might squeeze out performance—the zerocheck and logup batch kernels are taking the most time, followed by the monomial and interpolation kernels. But wait, I can't attribute these to MLE Rounds specifically since nsight doesn't have NVTX annotations for the MLE phase. Some of these kernel instances might be from recursion.\n\nAt APC 300 with 2 segments and ~12 rounds per segment = 24 total rounds:\n- zerocheck_batch_mle: should be 24 instances → matches\n- logup_batch_mle: should b