ed across multiple fold rounds. Looking at the fold_mle_evals kernel, I'm seeing that each iteration rebuilds these same metadata arrays from scratch, which is wasteful. If I precompute this structure upfront, I could eliminate that repeated work and potentially batch the H2D uploads more efficiently. I'm realizing the ping-pong buffer alternation is completely deterministic—if I know the initial addresses of both buffers, I can precompute every pointer for every round ahead of time. So instead of calculating input and output pointers during the loop, I'll generate all the descriptor arrays upfront, upload them once to the GPU, and then each iteration just references its precomputed slice.\n\nBut the view creation itself is necessary downstream—the sumcheck evaluation and subsequent folds need to query matrix dimensions and pointers. So I can't eliminate the views entirely, but I can precompute them all before the loop starts rather than creating them on-the-fly.\n\nThe real win is batching the descriptor work: instead of building and uploading fold descriptors per round, I'd compute all descriptor arrays upfront, pack them into one contiguous buffer, upload once, then have each round index into that precomputed data. I'd apply the same batching strategy to the interpolation descriptors for sumcheck evaluation.\n\nLooking at how fold_mle_evals creates non-owning views into the output buffer for foldable AIRs—those views get consumed by sumcheck_polys_batch_eval in the next round, so they're necessary. But I could precompute what those views will be rather than creating DeviceMatrix objects on the fly, or have sumcheck work directly from the precomputed buffer offsets instead.\n\nThe real win is eliminating the per-round fold descriptor building and H2D transfers. View creation itself is cheap—just pointer arithmetic and dimensions, no GPU work. So I should focus on precomputing the fold descriptors before the MLE loop: for each round, determine which mat_evals and sels entries are foldable, their dimensions, and output offsets in the ping-pong buffers. Since the data locations follow a predictable pattern through the ping-pong, I can precompute offsets within those buffers and upload everything upfront.\n\nThe tricky part is that input pointers for foldable AIRs come from either the previous round's fold output or the original data, and non-foldable AIRs keep frozen pointers to whichever buffer they last landed in—so the pattern isn't purely alternating. I need to track which buffer each AIR's data lives in after each round to fully precompute the input pointers. at whatever buffer it last landed in.\n\nThis makes full pre-computation of input_ptrs trickier. But here's the thing: the fold kernel only processes FOLDABLE AIRs. Non-foldable AIRs are not included in the descriptor array. So the input_ptrs for foldable AIRs in round r always come from the previous round's fold output buffer.\n\nFor round 0: foldable AIRs have input_ptrs from the original mat_evals (which are in the initial allocation)\nFor round 1: foldable AIRs have input_ptrs from round 0's output (buf_a or buf_b)\nFor round r: foldable AIRs have input_ptrs from round r-1's output\n\nSince the output of round r-1 is contiguously packed in the target buffer starting from offset 0, and we pre-computed the offsets, I can fully pre-compute all input and output pointers by alternating between two buffers across rounds. The source buffer for round r depends on whether r is even or odd, and the target is always the opposite buffer. Each foldable AIR's input pointer is just the source buffer base plus its pre-computed offset, and similarly for outputs.\n\nThere's a wrinkle though: in round 0, the foldable AIR inputs come from the original mat_evals, which might not follow the same layout as the fold output buffers. not be in either ping-pong buffer. The first fold reads from the original data locations.\n\nFrom the report on pingpong-mle-fold-buffers:\n> \"No initial D2D