p batch_mle kernels, monomial kernels, and interpolation—comes to around 70ms, which means the remaining 92ms is CPU and API overhead. That's substantial, and if I can eliminate 50-60% of it through pre-computation, I'm looking at 46-55ms in potential savings, though I should probably be more conservative and estimate 30-50ms to account for uncertainty in my breakdown.\n\nNow I'm examining sumcheck_polys_batch_eval more closely to see where else I can reduce overhead. The function has three distinct phases: metadata collection that runs on CPU, a batched interpolation kernel launch, and then trace context building with monomial evaluation kernels. Since this repeats every round, pre-computing the metadata across all rounds could unlock additional savings, though some aspects like the Case A vs Case B classification depend on the specific round.\n\nLooking at what's actually static versus dynamic: the interpolation descriptors and column dimensions are known upfront, but the column pointers shift after each fold operation. Rather than trying to pre-compute everything at once—which gets complicated fast—I should focus on the fold_mle_evals pre-computation first since that's the clearest optimization with the lowest implementation complexity. I can always layer in sumcheck_polys_batch_eval optimizations later if needed.\n\nActually, a simpler approach might work better: pre-compute just the static components like log heights, widths, and which AIRs are foldable per round, then fill in the dynamic parts like pointers during each round. This keeps the implementation manageable while still capturing most of the CPU savings.\n\nThe real insight is that checking which AIRs have height > 1 across all 623 of them every iteration is wasteful when we already know upfront which ones become non-foldable at each round. So I could pre-build round metadata containing the foldable indices and their properties, pre-allocate the GPU buffers, then during the loop just construct the pointer arrays for each round and upload those smaller transfers while the static data stays on the GPU.\n\nThe pointer building itself is still O(foldable AIRs per round), which approaches O(all AIRs) early on, but the key optimization is pre-computing the offsets within the ping-pong buffers so I can just add the base address at runtime rather than recalculating everything from scratch each iteration.\n\nDefining the metadata structure... Building pointer arrays from the pre-computed offsets and uploading them to the device, then launching the fold kernel with the pre-uploaded metadata before swapping buffers for the next iteration.\n\nNow I'm realizing round 0 needs special handling since its inputs come from scattered pointers in the original matrix evaluations, while subsequent rounds work with contiguous buffers. The elegant part is that for rounds >= 1, the input offsets are just the previous round's output offsets since we're reusing the same buffer after each swap—this keeps the pre-computation straightforward.\n\nThe pointer construction per round becomes straightforward: round 0 pulls inputs from the captured original pointers and outputs to buf_b, while later rounds source from whichever buffer holds the previous round's results and output to the alternate buffer. This approach is O(#foldable_AIRs) for building the pointer vectors and avoids the overhead of checking heights and rebuilding descriptors each iteration.\n\nThe real wins come from pre-computing what we can: foldability checks, log_heights, widths, and offsets all move off the critical path, cutting H2D transfers from 4 per round down to 2. That's roughly 17ms saved across 24 rounds—not huge, but the pointer construction itself still dominates since it scales with the number of AIRs.\n\nActually, I can pre-compute the pointers too since the ping-pong pattern is fixed and the offsets are known ahead of time.\n\nPre-computing pointer values...\n\nNow I'm uploading all the pointer arrays to the GPU in a single batch transfer, then during the fold loop each round just retrieves the pre-computed device pointers without any CPU overhead or additional H2D transfers. The only remaining per-round work is the kernel launch, updating the buffer views, and recording the transcript observation.\n\nThis optimization would save roughly 1ms per round across 24 rounds, totaling around 24ms just from fold_mle_evals. Combined with similar pre-computation for sumcheck_polys_batch_eval, I could potentially save 40-60ms overall. But I'm questioning whether 24ms alone justifies the added complexity, especially since the fold processes both the matrix evaluations and selector matrices, effectively doubling the overhead. I also need to consider pre-computing the DeviceMatrix views that get created after each fold for the sumcheck step, which would eliminate another CPU pass per round, though the Arc<DeviceBuffer> ownership model adds some constraints.\n\nLet me narrow this down to the most practical approach. Option A pre-computes all fold descriptors upfront, saving around 24ms with moderate complexity and low risk. Option B extends that to include sumcheck_polys metadata for roughly 50ms total savings but introduces higher complexity and more code changes. Option C involves multi-stream Merkle tree construction during trace commit, which is a different angle entirely.\n\nLooking at the trace commit path more carefully—the Explore agent mentioned 624 separate Merkle trees built sequentially, which seems like a huge opportunity if they're truly independent. But the kernel metrics show only 25 instances of the poseidon2 compressing kernel, which doesn't align with 624 trees unless they're being grouped somehow. I think the traces get stacked together before committing, so the individual AIR columns are being batched into fewer actual tree operations.\n\nIf there are maybe 5-8 distinct height groups per segment, then multi-streaming could parallelize those independent trees. But the GPU is probably already saturated with the single-stream hashing of these large groups, especially for APC 300 where the biggest group likely contains most of the columns.\n\nNow I'm looking at the actual numbers to find where I can shave off the 207ms gap. The components that don't scale with APC—like Round 0 at 173ms and MLE Rounds at 162ms—could each save 85-105ms if I could cut them in half, which would get me close to the target.\n\nRealistically though, I'm looking at maybe 60-100ms in savings from pre-computation and batched kernels, which still leaves me short of 1080ms. But the GKR fractional sumcheck is interesting—it's scaling almost linearly at 1.96x, which suggests there might be a bigger optimization opportunity there.\n\nLet me dig into the kernel breakdown for the fractional sumcheck itself. I'm seeing several compute-heavy kernels that are eating up around 233ms total across all segments, with the round computation and bit reversal kernels being the biggest culprits. If I could optimize those kernel calls or batch them more efficiently, that could be the 100+ millisecond win I'm looking for.\n\nFor just the app proof with its 2 segments and ~20 rounds each, the fractional sumcheck kernels would probably run closer to 100-150ms, and once I factor in CPU overhead from device-to-host transfers and transcript operations plus the per-round launch costs, I'm landing somewhere in the 200-300ms range. The input evaluation kernels are contributing around 60ms, and the setup/finalize work is taking up the remaining time to hit that 527ms total. But that setup/finalize number feels inflated—I should trace through where that time is actually going, especially since the tree building might be getting counted in multiple places. KR too:\n- bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel: 50ms (6 instances)\n- frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel: 12ms (66 instances)\n\nTotal tree build: ~62ms for the app proof portion.\n\nSo: input_eval (~60ms) + tree_build (~40ms) + fractional_sumcheck (~250ms) + setup/overhead (~177ms) = 527ms.\n\nThe 177ms of overhead is suspicious. It might include:\n- CPU-side work (descriptor building, transcript)\n- CUDA API overhead (kernel launches, D2H copies)\n- Memory allocation\n\nIf we could reduce this overhead, we might save 50-100ms on GKR.\n\nHmm, but the previous GKR optimization attempts (transcript processing, grid sizing, intermediates layout) all showed that the overhead is hard to reduce.\n\nLet me explore overlapping the stacked reduction with other phases. The stacked reduction takes 73ms and includes both Round 0 (block_sum_kernel at 22ms) and MLE rounds (fold_ple kernels at 11ms each). It's part of the Openings phase, which comes after Constraints, but Openings depends on constraint evaluation results so there's no overlap opportunity there. WHIR and stacked reduction are both in Openings and appear to be sequential anyway.\n\nI think the best path forward is pre-computing the MLE rounds — it's straightforward with a deterministic structure, moderate complexity, and should save 30-50ms with low risk since it doesn't require new CUDA kernels.\n\nBut let me double-check the math. Current measurements show APC 0 at 2160ms and APC 300 at 1287ms, giving a 1.68x ratio. The goal is to exceed 2x, meaning we need to get below 1080ms. A 40ms savings from MLE pre-computation would bring us to 1247ms, which is only a 1.73x ratio — still short of 2x. To actually hit 2x, I'd need to find another 167ms in savings, which might require tackling Round 0 batching or deeper GKR optimizations. The sequential protocol overhead is the real bottleneck here. Scoring these options by expected gain times probability, pre-computing MLE fold metadata comes out ahead with a score of 16 versus 7.5 for multi-stream Merkle trees and 4.7 for batching Round 0 kernels. That's my best next move. But before I commit to the plan, let me double-check the kernel execution times to see if there's a larger optimization I'm overlooking.\n\nLooking at the profiling data across all MLE phases, I'm noticing something striking: the `precompute_logup_combinations_kernel` is running 791 instances, which is far more than expected given the segment count.\n\nLet me recalculate: if we have around 400 AIRs with logup interactions across 2 segments, that gives us roughly 800 instances—which matches the 791 we're seeing. The combined GPU time for both kernel variants is about 55ms total, and since this precompute runs on a background stream overlapped with Round 0, it might not directly impact the critical path unless there's contention between streams.\n\nI should stop speculating about stream interference and focus on what's actually happening with the MLE rounds. Let me search the nsight kernel summary for the fold operations that correspond to `batch_fold_mle`. I'm looking for fold-related kernels that would handle the ping-pong reduction during the MLE phase.\n\nI found it — `plain_sumcheck::batch_fold_mle_kernel` is running at 11ms per call across 186 instances. Given the MLE structure with 24 rounds and multiple segments, I'd expect around 96 fold calls, but we're seeing 186 instances, likely due to recursive calls. That works out to roughly 0.06ms per kernel invocation, which is actually quite fast. The real issue is that the 162ms spent in MLE Rounds isn't coming from kernel execution time — it's something else entirely.\n\nBreaking down the kernel contributions: fold kernels account for maybe 6ms, batch_mle kernels around 24ms, monomial kernels about 15ms, totaling roughly 45ms of actual kernel work. That leaves 117ms unaccounted for, which is pure overhead — CPU work, CUDA API calls, memory transfers, and transcript operations. Spread across 24 rounds, that's nearly 5ms of overhead per round. If I can cut that overhead in half through pre-computation, I'm looking at roughly 60ms in savings, which would be substantial. The overhead likely comes from CPU-side descriptor building for the 623 AIRs and memory transfer costs.\n\nLooking at where the overhead actually breaks down per round: fold descriptor building and uploads cost about 1.3ms, while sumcheck metadata collection and transfers add another 2.5ms. If I pre-compute and cache the fold descriptors, that's 31ms saved across all rounds. Pre-computing the sumcheck metadata too would save another 60ms. That's a meaningful optimization opportunity.\n\nBut I'm realizing the sumcheck path is more complex than I initially thought—it touches interpolation and monomial evaluation with tighter dependencies. So I should focus on just the fold descriptor pre-computation for now, which should realistically save 25-35ms with much lower implementation risk.\n\nActually, let me double-check my math against the actual profiling data. The nsight results show 30,069 H2D operations consuming 333ms total and 3.7GB across the entire proof. For the MLE rounds specifically at APC 300 with 24 rounds, the fold_mle_evals phase is doing 4 uploads per round.\n\nLooking at the actual kernel execution times for the app proof MLE rounds at APC 300 with 2 segments and roughly 12 rounds per segment, I need to trace through what each round is actually launching to get a clearer picture of where the real bottleneck is.\n\nEach round runs several kernels: the fold operations for matrix evaluations and selectors take about 0.06ms each, interpolating columns takes around 0.1ms, the monomial evaluations add up to roughly 0.5ms, then zerocheck and logup batch operations contribute another 1ms and 0.5ms respectively. That puts me at about 2.2ms of actual kernel execution per round. Across 24 rounds, that's only 53ms of GPU work, which means the remaining 109ms is overhead—mostly from the GPU sitting idle between kernel launches and the driver call overhead itself. With around 144 kernel launches total, even at just 20-30 microseconds per launch, that adds up quickly.\n\nThe real culprit is likely the CPU-side work: building descriptors for each round, copying data to and from the GPU, and all the Rust allocations and Arc operations. If I could reduce the descriptor building from scaling with the number of matrix evaluations down to a constant-time operation, I'd reclaim most of that 105ms overhead. Let me think through exactly how much time the descriptor building is consuming by looking at what happens each round when iterating through the matrix evaluations.\n\nBuilding descriptor arrays... Building column pointer arrays and trace context structures is the real bottleneck—with 106K total columns across all AIRs, constructing those pointers takes roughly 1ms per round, which scales to 24ms across all rounds. The per-AIR classification and descriptor building are relatively cheap by comparison.\n\nThe H2D transfer of those 850KB of pointers adds another 0.2ms per round, and when I factor in the trace context and monomial metadata construction, the total overhead climbs to about 1.8ms per round—43ms across the full sumcheck. This scales directly with column count, so it's much worse at higher APC values. Pre-computing these structures across all rounds at once could be a significant optimization.\n\nThe tricky part is that column pointers shift after each fold operation since the trace buffers get rearranged in the ping-pong pattern. But since I've already tracked where each column ends up after every fold, I should be able to pre-compute all the pointer arrays upfront. That could save roughly 43ms from sumcheck plus another 1.2ms from fold overhead—around 44ms total, which combined with reducing CUDA API calls might get me close to 47ms of savings.\n\nStill, that's not quite enough to hit the 207ms target. Let me reconsider whether the 162ms for MLE rounds already includes the logup combination precomputation.\n\nActually, the Explore agent clarified that logup monomial precomputation runs once before the MLE loop on a background thread during Round 0, so it shouldn't be counted in the MLE Rounds timing. Looking at the actual tracing span for MLE Rounds, it should cover the main loop where sumcheck polys are evaluated, fold operations happen, and transcripts are updated. Let me trace through the code to see exactly what's included in that measurement.\n\nFrom the MLE agent's response, each round in the main loop does sumcheck polynomial batch evaluation, computes the batch s polynomial, evaluates and observes sumcheck evals, samples a transcript value, and then folds the MLE evaluations. So the 162ms total includes all of these operations across 24 rounds, which works out to about 6.75ms per round. Given that kernel execution is around 2.2ms, that leaves roughly 4.5ms of overhead per round.\n\nThe optimization targets I'm looking at would save about 2ms per round by precomputing fold descriptors, reducing H2D transfers for fold operations, precomputing sumcheck column pointers and their transfers, and precomputing the sumcheck interpolation column descriptions and trace context with monomials.\n\nLooking at the next chunk, I realize the column pointer building is actually the dominant cost at around 1ms per round because we're looking up pointers for 106K columns. But here's the key insight: after folding, only the columns that actually get folded have their pointers change. The non-foldable columns keep the same pointers throughout all rounds.\n\nSo instead of rebuilding the entire column pointer array each round, I could precompute all the arrays upfront and account for which columns fold when, or use a delta approach where I only update the entries that actually changed. The delta approach would be much faster since later rounds have fewer foldable columns.\n\nI'm realizing this is getting complicated though, so I need to step back and figure out which optimization would actually have the most impact.\n\nWait, I'm noticing something in the kernel profiling data. The monomial kernels are being called way more frequently than expected—around 9 times per round instead of just 1-2 times. This suggests the monomial evaluation is being split into multiple sub-batches based on memory constraints rather than running as a single batch per round.\n\nWith 623 AIRs and a memory budget that fits roughly 70-100 AIRs per batch, that would create 6-9 sub-batches per round. Across 24 MLE rounds, that's about 216 kernel launches total, which matches the observed 221 instances. Each sub-batch incurs its own overhead for descriptor building, GPU uploads, and kernel launches—and while the descriptor building itself is minimal per AIR, the cumulative effect across all these sub-batches is where the hidden cost lies.\n\nThe real bottlenecks are likely elsewhere: the Arc operations when updating views after folding, the CPU work of building TraceCtx objects with all their column pointers for each AIR, and the repeated allocation and deallocation of large vectors across the 24 rounds. Rather than continuing to analyze, I should just commit to the pre-computed fold and eval metadata optimization and write out the implementation plan.\n\nLooking at the MLE Rounds timing at APC 0 though—113ms with 99 AIRs and ~4K columns—the per-round overhead there is minimal, around 0.05ms, so kernel execution is the real bottleneck at that level. The 49ms gap between wall time and kernel time at higher APCs is entirely coming from the per-AIR and per-column overhead, which confirms where the optimization effort should focus.\n\nNow I need to figure out the actual number of MLE rounds at APC 300. The trace heights determine this, and with 623 AIRs they're likely smaller than at APC 0, so maybe around 17 rounds instead of 21. But the spec output showed 162ms for MLE Rounds at APC 300, so let me work backwards from that to understand the actual round count.\n\nActually, n_max is just the maximum log₂ of trace height across all AIRs in a segment. Even with many small AIRs at APC 300, the common ones like memory and range checker are still present, so the maximum height stays substantial.\n\nRather than getting bogged down in exact numbers, let me focus on the optimization itself: pre-computing MLE fold descriptors for all rounds upfront and uploading them once before the loop. This should save 25-40ms at APC 300 by eliminating per-round descriptor building and host-to-device transfers. It's a high-probability win since the structure is deterministic, though the complexity is moderate.\n\nThat said, 25-40ms is decent but not huge. Let me think about whether there's a bigger bottleneck I should be targeting instead, maybe in the Trace Commit path.\n\nLooking at the Merkle tree construction more carefully—they're built per height-group rather than per AIR, so there are probably only 5-10 groups per segment. The Poseidon2 hashing kernel appears roughly 25 times across all phases, with maybe 10-15 instances in the app proof alone. If each instance takes around 10ms, that's roughly 100ms just for Poseidon2 hashing. Combined with other operations like zero hash calculation, the 246ms Trace Commit time starts to make more sense.\n\nThe batch stacking optimization already cut this down from 406ms to 255ms, which is a solid 2.16x improvement. Within that 246ms, the major contributors are RS encoding (which expands trace columns via NTT), row hashing, layer compression, and zero hash calculation. Rather than drilling deeper into each component, the key takeaway is that Trace Commit at APC 300 is already significantly faster than at APC 0.\n\nThe real bottleneck is elsewhere—specifically in the components that don't scale well: Round 0, MLE Rounds, and Stacked Reduction. MLE Rounds is the worst performer with a 0.70x ratio, meaning it actually gets slower at APC 300 compared to APC 0. That's where I need to focus.\n\nI'm considering a more aggressive optimization: a persistent kernel that runs all MLE folding rounds in a single kernel launch instead of spawning 24 separate kernels. This would eliminate the launch overhead and let the kernel fold all applicable AIRs for each round sequentially on the GPU.\n\nThe approach could save 60-80ms by cutting out kernel launch overhead, descriptor uploads, and CPU descriptor building, though it requires managing GPU-side state and handling the varying number of foldable AIRs across rounds.\n\nI'm leaning toward a middle-ground strategy instead: pre-compute all fold descriptors and sumcheck metadata upfront, upload once, then keep the per-round kernel launches minimal. This avoids the complexity and synchronization risks of persistent kernels while still eliminating most of the CPU descriptor building overhead—probably saving around 50-70ms with much lower engineering risk.\n\nSince all the metadata is deterministic based on round number and `n_lift`, I can pre-compute everything: fold indices and output offsets, evaluation column descriptors and pointers, and trace contexts. The column pointers after each fold are also predictable since I know where the ping-pong buffers will point. So the entire loop becomes just launching kernels with pre-computed parameters and handling transcripts.\n\nNow I'm working through the specifics of what gets pre-computed for each round—determining which matrices are foldable based on their initial heights, calculating where the folded outputs land in memory, and tracking how heights shrink after each fold operation. Now I'm working through the metadata setup for the evaluation phase, specifically how to map column pointers across rounds depending on whether they're foldable or not. This gets intricate with the ping-pong buffer offsets, but I'm going to scope this down and focus on just the fold pre-computation for now—the eval metadata can be handled in a follow-up.\n\nA 25-35ms improvement from fold pre-computation alone is solid and clears the typical rollback threshold. But I'm realizing there's a much more efficient approach: instead of building descriptor arrays per round, I could pre-compute which AIRs are foldable at each round and store all descriptors contiguously in GPU memory, since AIRs drop out of the foldable set in predictable groups based on their initial heights. AIRs with initial height 2^k become non-foldable at round k. So:\n\n- Round 1: all AIRs with height >= 2 are foldable\n- Round 2: all AIRs with height >= 4 are foldable\n- Round k: all AIRs with height >= 2^k are foldable\n\nWe can sort AIRs by height and for each round, take a prefix of the sorted list.\n\nThen we build descriptors for ALL foldable AIRs across ALL rounds as one big contiguous array:\n- [round 0 descriptors | round 1 descriptors | ... | round n_max-1 descriptors]\n\nEach round's segment has fewer entries than the previous (as AIRs drop out).\n\nWe upload this entire array once. During the loop, each round just references its segment via an offset and count.\n\nThis is simple, clean, and eliminates all per-round CPU work and H2D uploads for the fold operation.\n\nThe height data and output offsets are all pre-computed. For input pointers, round 0 reads from the original scattered locations which we pre-store, while later rounds read from the previous round's output buffer. Since output offsets are pre-computed, I can derive round r's inputs directly from the buffer base plus the pre-computed offset from round r-1. The source buffer alternates between two buffers, so I'll compute input pointers on the fly using the pre-computed offsets and whichever buffer is currently active. I'm realizing I can pre-compute two complete pointer arrays for each round—one for each buffer base—and upload them both, then just select the right one at runtime. This trades a bit of extra upload space (around 600KB total across all rounds) for eliminating per-round pointer array work entirely. So the strategy becomes: pre-compute all fold descriptors upfront,\n\npack them into one contiguous buffer, do a single H2D upload, then during the MLE loop each round just indexes into the pre-uploaded data and launches the kernel with those pre-computed parameters. Looking at the actual per-round overhead at APC 300, I'm seeing CPU iteration for descriptor building takes about 0.05ms, vector allocation/deallocation adds 0.01ms, the four H2D uploads cost roughly 0.15ms in CUDA API overhead alone, and view updates for DeviceMatrix objects run about 0.3ms.\n\nThat's roughly 0.5ms per round, which across 24 rounds is only 12ms total. But wait—the view update is unavoidable regardless, and the H2D is just 0.15ms per round. So pre-computing the fold descriptors would save maybe 0.05ms on descriptor building plus 0.15ms on uploads, totaling around 5ms across all rounds. That's not nearly enough to explain the 109ms overhead I'm seeing between the 162ms total MLE time and 53ms of actual kernel execution.\n\nI need to look deeper at where the real bottleneck is—4.5ms per round is still unaccounted for.\n\nThe fold-related work is only about 0.5ms per round, so the remaining 4ms must be coming from sumcheck_polys_batch_eval and related operations. Looking at that function, the metadata collection phase iterates through all 623 AIRs to classify them as Case A or B, then for Case B it collects column pointers from 106K total columns into a flat array. That pointer collection alone is probably taking 1-2ms per round.\n\nThe batched interpolation phase uploads descriptors and column pointers to the GPU (about 850KB total), which takes roughly 0.3ms including kernel launch overhead. Then for monomial evaluation, I'm building a TraceCtx for each Case B AIR and splitting the work into memory-aware sub-batches, which involves multiple H2D uploads and kernel launches totaling around 1.2-1.5ms. The D2H copies for the evaluated values add another 0.2ms, and then there's the transcript observe and sample operations that still need to be accounted for.\n\nTallying everything up—the fold pre-computation, metadata collection in phase 1, monomial batch building in phase 3, and all the data transfers—I'm getting roughly 4ms per round