se A traces are excluded. If I maintain a persistent array with all traces' columns, the offsets for Case B traces would need to account for the Case A entries in between.\n\nLet me reconsider the tradeoffs. Looking at the actual overhead breakdown at APC 300, the column collection phase takes about 38ms, per-trace work is 24ms, and descriptor setup plus data transfer is 12ms. The remaining 35ms goes to other operations. I should focus on the approach that gives the best improvement without introducing too much complexity.\n\nActually, I'm realizing I might be overcomplicating this. Let me step back and think about whether there's a simpler optimization entirely outside the MLE Rounds pipeline that could have more impact. But the task is asking for a single optimization idea, so I need to pick the one with the best risk-to-reward ratio.\n\nLet me look at the column collection code more carefully...\n\nExamining the column pointer collection logic...\n\nThe key insight is that we're computing per-column pointers for every single column across all matrices, which involves stride calculations repeated for each column. What if instead we passed per-matrix metadata to the kernel and let it compute the column offsets internally? That way we'd only collect a few base pointers and dimensions rather than thousands of individual column pointers.\n\nDefining the matrix descriptor structure... I'm realizing the descriptor approach cuts the H2D upload from ~850KB down to just 29KB by storing per-matrix metadata instead of per-column pointers, saving about 38ms at APC 300 with minimal kernel overhead. The tradeoff is needing to modify the CUDA kernel, update the descriptor format, add new FFI bindings, and change the Rust caller—moderate complexity overall since the kernel change is straightforward.\n\nAn even simpler optimization would be to compute column pointers directly in the kernel from per-matrix base pointers and strides rather than looking them up in a flat array, which