ust uploading ~1800 matrix descriptors instead.\n\nThe new kernel will accept per-matrix base pointers and compute column addresses on the fly using `base + col_within_matrix * height`. I'm introducing a descriptor format that stores per-trace output pointers and dimensions, plus per-matrix base pointers and widths, all packed into a flat array. On the Rust side, I'll build these descriptors instead of the column pointer Vec, upload the much smaller descriptor buffer (32KB vs 850KB), and call the optimized kernel.\n\nLooking at the performance impact, I'm estimating around 1.0-1.6ms CPU savings per round and 0.1ms H2D savings per round, which across ~24 rounds at APC 300 translates to roughly 27-40ms total improvement. That would bring MLE Rounds down from 162ms to around 122-135ms, and the full STARK trace computation from 1287ms to 1247-1260ms. The risks are minimal—APC 0 won't regress since it only has 4K columns, the address computation is just a multiply and add per thread, and the per-matrix loop is short. But I should verify these time estimates more carefully.\n\nLet me reconsider the column pointer collection overhead. I'm looking at the cost of loading pointers from the DeviceMatrix struct, computing addresses with a multiply-add, and pushing to a vector, plus the iterator overhead from chaining and flat-mapping across matrices. The iterator abstraction in Rust compiles efficiently but there's still per-element function call overhead. A more conservative estimate accounting for this would be 20-30ns per column iteration, which for 106K columns per round gives 2.1-3.2ms—higher than my initial 1.6ms assumption.\n\nBut not every round processes the full 106K columns. As rounds progress, fewer traces are Case B, so the workload decreases significantly. Across all 24 rounds, I'm looking at roughly 800K to 1.2M total column iterations, which at 25ns each comes to about 20-30ms total savings. That's still meaningful and the optimization is straightforward and low