{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RgTbDNkVSN1QJn7Z5kKKtj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0138K1DzfNm1wL5BTD34wmGp","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-14-2000-matrix-base-ptr-interpolation\n\nReplace the flat per-column pointer array (~106K entries at APC 300) with per-matrix base pointers (~2K entries) in the batched interpolation kernel used during MLE rounds. Currently, each MLE round rebuilds a `Vec<*const EF>` of all column GPU addresses by iterating O(#total_columns) on the CPU (~1.6ms per round), then uploads it H2D (850KB). By passing per-matrix base pointers and widths instead, the kernel computes column addresses internally via `base + col * height`, reducing the CPU-side collection from O(106K) to O(2K) and the H2D from 850KB to ~40KB per round.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1868,"cache_read_input_tokens":149360,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1868},"output_tokens":65,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ae9b2456-7a6a-41ad-a41d-1e3f4310411a","uuid":"225a82cb-fc60-422c-bfb7-d5464d7ba941"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0138K1DzfNm1wL5BTD34wmGp","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ae9b2456-7a6a-41ad-a41d-1e3f4310411a","uuid":"f21e93b7-9dda-450c-9f15-b7efa7f39f1f","timestamp":"2026-04-14T17:42:41.104Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-14-2000-matrix-base-ptr-interpolation\n\nReplace the flat per-column pointer array (~106K entries at APC 300) with per-matrix base pointers (~2K entries) in the batched interpolation kernel used during MLE rounds. Currently, each MLE round rebuilds a `Vec<*const EF>` of all column GPU addresses by iterating O(#total_columns) on the CPU (~1.6ms per round), then uploads it H2D (850KB). By passing per-matrix base pointers and widths instead, the kernel computes column addresses internally via `base + col * height`, reducing the CPU-side collection from O(106K) to O(2K) and the H2D from 850KB to ~40KB per round.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1,"newLines":3,"lines":["-# 2026-04-14-1800-gpu-gkr-transcript-processing","+# 2026-04-14-2000-matrix-base-ptr-interpolation"," ","-Eliminate per-round CPU-GPU roundtrips in the GKR fractional sumcheck by moving the transcript observe/sample operations to GPU. Currently, each GKR sumcheck round launches a GPU kernel, then does a blocking D2H copy of 2 field elements, CPU-side arithmetic (reconstruct_s_evals), CPU-side Poseidon2 transcript absorb/squeeze, and finally launches the next round's kernel. With ~20 rounds per segment × 2 segments = ~40 roundtrips, nsight profiling shows 14ms of inter-kernel gap per segment (28ms total) from these CPU-GPU transitions. By implementing a small GPU kernel that fuses the post-round processing (reconstruct, sponge observe, sponge squeeze, accumulator update), we eliminate all mid-loop D2H copies and keep the entire GKR round loop on the GPU, with a single D2H at the end to retrieve the final transcript state.","+Replace the flat per-column pointer array (~106K entries at APC 300) with per-matrix base pointers (~2K entries) in the batched interpolation kernel used during MLE rounds. Currently, each MLE round rebuilds a `Vec<*const EF>` of all column GPU addresses by iterating O(#total_columns) on the CPU (~1.6ms per round), then uploads it H2D (850KB). By passing per-matrix base pointers and widths instead, the kernel computes column addresses internally via `base + col * height`, reducing the CPU-side collection from O(106K) to O(2K) and the H2D from 850KB to ~40KB per round."]}],"originalFile":"# 2026-04-14-1800-gpu-gkr-transcript-processing\n\nEliminate per-round CPU-GPU roundtrips in the GKR fractional sumcheck by moving the transcript observe/sample operations to GPU. Currently, each GKR sumcheck round launches a GPU kernel, then does a blocking D2H copy of 2 field elements, CPU-side arithmetic (reconstruct_s_evals), CPU-side Poseidon2 transcript absorb/squeeze, and finally launches the next round's kernel. With ~20 rounds per segment × 2 segments = ~40 roundtrips, nsight profiling shows 14ms of inter-kernel gap per segment (28ms total) from these CPU-GPU transitions. By implementing a small GPU kernel that fuses the post-round processing (reconstruct, sponge observe, sponge squeeze, accumulator update), we eliminate all mid-loop D2H copies and keep the entire GKR round loop on the GPU, with a single D2H at the end to retrieve the final transcript state.\n"}}
