{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0125pm5E91psKc6dxm8VpFvz","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Batch MLE Per-Trace H2D Copies\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReplace ~5000 per-trace `main_ptrs.to_device()` H2D copies per segment in MLE `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` with ~24 bulk per-round uploads. This reduces CUDA API overhead from ~25ms per segment to ~1ms per segment, targeting a 40-50ms improvement on MLE Rounds at APC 300 (currently 162ms).\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\tThe bottleneck is in `LogupZerocheckGpu::sumcheck_polys_batch_eval()` at `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:1575-1961`. This function is called 24 times per segment (once per MLE round) at APC 300.\n10\t\n11\t### Call chain\n12\t```\n13\tMLE Rounds loop (mod.rs:659)\n14\t  └─ sumcheck_polys_batch_eval(round, r_prev) (mod.rs:1575)\n15\t       ├─ Phase 1: Classify all 623 traces (mod.rs:1590-1725)\n16\t       │    ├─ Case A.1 (round == n_lift+1): build Vec<MainMatrixPtrs>, to_device() per trace (mod.rs:1652-1659)\n17\t       │    ├─ Case A.2 (round > n_lift+1): scale tilde evals only (mod.rs:1678-1686)\n18\t       │    └─ Case B (round <= n_lift): collect column pointers (mod.rs:1688-1724)\n19\t       ├─ Phase 2: Batched interpolation kernel (mod.rs:1727-1765) — already 1 H2D per round\n20\t       ├─ Phase 3: Build TraceCtx for Case B traces (mod.rs:1767-1828)\n21\t       │    └─ Per trace: build Vec<MainMatrixPtrs>, to_device() (mod.rs:1799-1811) ← BOTTLENECK\n22\t       └─ Phase 4: Batch evaluations (mod.rs:1830-1958)\n23\t```\n24\t\n25\t### Why it's slow\n26\t\n27\t- Phase 3 calls `main_ptrs.to_device()` once per Case B trace per round.\n28\t- At round 1, all 623 traces are Case B → 623 H2D copies.\n29\t- At round 12, ~300 traces are still Case B → 300 H2D copies.\n30\t- Across 24 rounds for segment 0, nsight measures **5168 H2D copies** totaling 40MB.\n31\t- Each call invokes `cudaMemcpyAsync` (confirmed median ~1.8μs per call from nsight `cuda_api_sum`) plus host-side Vec allocation.\n32\t- Phase 1 Case A.1 adds another ~623 H2D copies across all rounds (one per trace at its transition round).\n33\t- Total per segment: ~5800 H2D API calls contributing ~25ms of CPU-side overhead.\n34\t\n35\t### Data structures\n36\t\n37\t`TraceCtx` (batch_mle.rs:89-105) holds:\n38\t```rust\n39\tpub main_ptrs_dev: DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>,\n40\t```\n41\tEach trace's `main_ptrs_dev` is an independently-allocated device buffer containing 1-5 `MainMatrixPtrs` structs (16 bytes each: pointer + width). Consumers access it via:\n42\t- `t.main_ptrs_dev.as_ptr()` — used in batch builder context structs (batch_mle_monomial.rs:184,407,672; batch_mle.rs:188,335)\n43\t- `&t.main_ptrs_dev` — passed by reference to `evaluate_mle_constraints_gpu` and `evaluate_mle_interactions_gpu` (mle_round.rs:28,82), which only call `.as_ptr()` and `.len()` on it.\n44\t\n45\t## Changes\n46\t\n47\t### Change 1: Batch Phase 3 (Case B) main_ptrs uploads\n48\t\n49\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n50\t\n51\tCurrently (lines 1767-1828):\n52\t```rust\n53\tfor meta in &case_b_traces {\n54\t    // ... compute pointers ...\n55\t    let main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = mats[first_main_idx..].iter().map(|m| { ... }).collect();\n56\t    let main_ptrs_dev = main_ptrs.to_device()?;\n57\t    early_eval.push(TraceCtx { ..., main_ptrs_dev, ... });\n58\t}\n59\t```\n60\t\n61\tChange to:\n62\t```rust\n63\t// Step 1: Collect ALL MainMatrixPtrs for all Case B traces into one Vec, tracking per-trace offsets\n64\tlet mut all_main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = Vec::new();\n65\tlet mut main_ptrs_offsets: Vec<(usize, usize)> = Vec::new(); // (offset, count) per case_b trace\n66\t\n67\tfor meta in &case_b_traces {\n68\t    let mats = &self.mat_evals_per_trace[meta.trace_idx];\n69\t    let first_main_idx = usize::from(meta.has_preprocessed);\n70\t    let offset = all_main_ptrs.len();\n71\t    all_main_ptrs.extend(mats[first_main_idx..].iter().map(|m| {\n72\t        let interpolated_height = sp_deg * meta.num_y;\n73\t        MainMatrixPtrs {\n74\t            data: base_ptr.wrapping_add(widths_so_far * interpolated_height),\n75\t            air_width: air_width_for_mat(meta.need_rot, m.width()),\n76\t        }\n77\t        // NOTE: widths_so_far tracking must be done in the loop body, same as current code\n78\t    }));\n79\t    let count = all_main_ptrs.len() - offset;\n80\t    main_ptrs_offsets.push((offset, count));\n81\t}\n82\t\n83\t// Step 2: Single bulk H2D upload\n84\tlet d_all_main_ptrs = if !all_main_ptrs.is_empty() {\n85\t    Some(all_main_ptrs.to_device()?)\n86\t} else {\n87\t    None\n88\t};\n89\t\n90\t// Step 3: Create TraceCtx with non-owning views into the bulk buffer\n91\tfor (meta, &(offset, count)) in case_b_traces.iter().zip(main_ptrs_offsets.iter()) {\n92\t    let main_ptrs_dev = unsafe {\n93\t        DeviceBuffer::non_owning(\n94\t            d_all_main_ptrs.as_ref().unwrap().as_mut_ptr().add(offset),\n95\t            count,\n96\t        )\n97\t    };\n98\t    // ... compute prep_ptr, sels_ptr, etc. (unchanged) ...\n99\t    early_eval.push(TraceCtx { ..., main_ptrs_dev, ... });\n100\t}\n101\t```\n102\t\n103\tThe `d_all_main_ptrs` buffer is kept alive alongside `_keepalive_interpolated` until the end of the function. All non-owning views in `early_eval` TraceCtx remain valid.\n104\t\n105\t### Change 2: Batch Phase 1 Case A.1 main_ptrs uploads\n106\t\n107\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n108\t\n109\tCurrently (lines 1639-1675): Each Case A.1 trace does `main_ptrs.to_device()` individually.\n110\t\n111\tApply the same pattern:\n112\t```rust\n113\t// After the Phase 1 loop: collect all Case A.1 main_ptrs, bulk upload, create non-owning views\n114\tlet mut all_late_main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = Vec::new();\n115\tlet mut late_offsets: Vec<(usize, usize)> = Vec::new();\n116\t\n117\t// During Phase 1 Case A.1, instead of to_device(), append to all_late_main_ptrs and record offset\n118\t```\n119\t\n120\tRestructure the Phase 1 loop to collect Case A.1 main_ptrs into a Vec (alongside their other TraceCtx fields stored in a temporary struct), then do a single bulk upload, then create TraceCtx objects with non-owning views.\n121\t\n122\tThis requires splitting the Phase 1 Case A.1 branch into two passes:\n123\t1. First pass: collect main_ptrs data + temporary metadata into `Vec<CaseA1Meta>` (no H2D)\n124\t2. After loop: single bulk `all_late_main_ptrs.to_device()` \n125\t3. Second pass: create `TraceCtx` objects with non-owning views\n126\t\n127\t### Change 3: Keep bulk buffers alive\n128\t\n129\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n130\t\n131\tAdd `_keepalive_early_main_ptrs` and `_keepalive_late_main_ptrs` variables (similar pattern to existing `_keepalive_interpolated`) to ensure the bulk DeviceBuffers outlive all non-owning views.\n132\t\n133\tBoth variables must be declared before the TraceCtx vectors and dropped after all batch evaluation functions complete (end of `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`).\n134\t\n135\t### Change 4: DeviceBuffer::as_mut_ptr() for offset arithmetic\n136\t\n137\t**File**: `crates/cuda-common/src/d_buffer.rs`\n138\t\n139\tCheck if `as_mut_ptr()` exists on DeviceBuffer. If not, add it (trivially: return `self.ptr`). Needed for computing non-owning view pointers with `.add(offset)`.\n140\t\n141\tAlternatively, use `as_ptr() as *mut T` since the non_owning constructor takes `*mut T`.\n142\t\n143\t## Invariants\n144\t\n145\t1. **Lifetime safety**: All non-owning DeviceBuffer views in TraceCtx must be consumed (batch evaluations complete, including D2H copies) before the backing bulk buffer is dropped. The existing function structure guarantees this: bulk buffers are declared at function scope, TraceCtx vectors are consumed by Phase 4 batch evaluations, and the function returns after Phase 4.\n146\t\n147\t2. **Pointer correctness**: Non-owning view pointers must point to the correct sub-region of the bulk buffer. The offset arithmetic (`base_ptr.add(offset)`) must match the order in which MainMatrixPtrs were appended to the bulk Vec.\n148\t\n149\t3. **No APC 0 regression**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs, 5 segments, ~20 AIRs per segment), the overhead reduction is proportionally smaller but the bulk allocation overhead is also smaller. The optimization should be neutral or slightly positive at APC 0.\n150\t\n151\t4. **Correctness of evaluation results**: The batch evaluation functions (LogupMonomialBatch, ZerocheckMonomialBatch, etc.) access `t.main_ptrs_dev.as_ptr()` to read MainMatrixPtrs from device memory. The non-owning view provides the same pointer, so GPU kernels see identical data. The `.len()` return value must also match (the non-owning constructor sets len correctly).\n152\t\n153\t5. **No change to CUDA kernels**: This optimization is pure Rust-side; no CUDA kernel code changes.\n154\t\n155\t## Measurement Plan\n156\t\n157\tRun the standard benchmark:\n158\t```bash\n159\tcd /home/georg/powdr/results/pairing\n160\t/home/georg/powdr/target/release/powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics <output_path> --recursion\n161\t```\n162\t\n163\tFor APC 0:\n164\t```bash\n165\t/home/georg/powdr/target/release/powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics <output_path> --recursion\n166\t```\n167\t\n168\tAnalyze with `python3 /home/georg/spec.py <metrics_path> <name>`.\n169\t\n170\tRun nsight profiling for APC 300 to verify H2D count reduction:\n171\t```bash\n172\tnsys profile --output <output> --force-overwrite true --trace cuda,nvtx,osrt --sample none --stats true -- <binary> prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --recursion\n173\t```\n174\t\n175\t### Expected results\n176\t- MLE Rounds at APC 300: 162ms → 112-125ms (25-40% reduction)\n177\t- STARK excl trace at APC 300: 1296ms → 1246-1260ms\n178\t- H2D copy count in MLE window: ~5168 → ~200-400 per segment\n179\t- No regression at APC 0 (MLE Rounds stays ~113ms)\n180\t\n181\t## Rollback Criteria\n182\t\n183\tRevert if:\n184\t- MLE Rounds improvement at APC 300 is less than 15ms (below measurement noise threshold)\n185\t- Any regression at APC 0 exceeds 10ms on STARK excl trace\n186\t- Proof verification fails at any APC configuration\n187\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XQFBpZ3Zrmu9Qka2ScTing","session_id":"ae9b2456-7a6a-41ad-a41d-1e3f4310411a","uuid":"7626542d-0867-469f-954e-dd374334e531","timestamp":"2026-04-14T17:54:02.361Z"}
