ding matrices once and indexing by round\n\nIf the set of Case B traces changes predictably per round (it does: traces transition from Case B to Case A as `round` crosses their `n_lift`), one could pre-upload a superset of matrix infos and vary only the descriptors per round. This would further reduce H2D to near-zero for subsequent rounds. However, given the total savings target is only 8-12ms, the added complexity is probably not justified.\n\n## Assessment\n\nThere is no design-level correctness blocker. The column address computation in the new kernel is provably equivalent to the current code (`base + col * height` where `height = 2 * num_y`). The FFI types follow the established pattern used by the existing `InterpColDesc`. Phase 3 and its invariants are correctly left untouched.\n\nThe plan is prototype-ready. All structs, kernel code, FFI bindings, and Rust-side orchestration are fully specified. The only implementation-time decision is preserving the per-matrix height assertion (finding 1), which is trivial.\n\nThe strongest part of the plan is the savings estimate. It is grounded in per-element timing analysis (6-10ns per column), cross-referenced against three prior MLE optimization results that bracket the expected improvement, and the rollback threshold is appropriately calibrated at 5ms rather than the default 10ms. The honest acknowledgment that prior work showed per-call CUDA API overhead is only ~1us (ruling out the H2D reduction as the primary savings mechanism) and the correct identification of CPU iteration as the dominant cost distinguish this plan from earlier MLE optimization plans that overestimated CUDA API overhead."}],"totalDurationMs":118037,"totalTokens":45187,"totalToolUseCount":23,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":214,"cache_read_input_tokens":42901,"output_tokens":2071,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephe